href "/venues/2173"} "Grand Palais Éphémère, Paris, France"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/2007262"} "24 from 24 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/129806"} "Barbara Matić"] " of Croatia was world ranked #1 coming into Paris 2024. She was the 2021 and 2022 World Champion and had won the recent 2024 European Championship. The defending gold medalist was Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/144397"} "Chizuru Arai"] ", but she had retired and would not defend the title. Ranked second in the world was Dutchwoman " [:a {:href "/athletes/145504"} "Sanne van Dijke"] ", who won a bronze medal in the class at Tokyo 2020 and added bronze medals at the 2021 and 2022 World Championships."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Matić was unchallenged in Paris, winning all her early rounds by " [:em {} "ippon"] ", and then defeating Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/153264"} "Miriam Butkereit"] " by " [:em {} "waza-ari"] " in the final, in a somewhat uneventful match."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Van Dijke lost her semi-final to Matić, and then failed to win a medal when she lost her bronze medal match against Belgium’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/153203"} "Gabriella Willems"] ". The other bronze medal went to Austrian " [:a {:href "/athletes/2101235"} "Michaela Polleres"] ", who defeated Spain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/153240"} "Ai Tsunoda"] " by " [:em {} "ippon"] ", using " [:em {} "sumi-gaeshi"] " to win at 1:47. Tsunoda had been born in Spain to a Japanese father and a French mother."] "\n\n" [:p {} "At the 2024 World Championships, in May, French judokas went 1-2 in this class, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/142748"} "Margaux Pinot"] " winning gold and " [:a {:href "/athletes/153250"} "Marie-Ève Gahié"] " taking silver. Gahié was already chosen as the French representative in the class by the selection committee in February 2024, but she lost her quarter-final in Paris to Polleres, and lost again to Willems in the repêchage round, both by " [:em {} 